<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anyang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anyang is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, near the site of Yin, the last capital of the Shang dynasty. Excavations there since 1928 have uncovered royal tombs, palace foundations and tens of thousands of inscribed oracle bones, the earliest Chinese writing. The ruins of Yin became a World Heritage Site in 2006.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anyang.jpg" alt="Anyang" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Windmemories · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20250831_Temple_of_Han_Qi_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tradition records frequent moves of the royal capital, and a large walled city at Huanbei, identified in 1999 just north of <b>Anyang</b>, may represent one stage of them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its earliest written records are the oracle-bone inscriptions of the Shang dynasty, carved from about 1250 BCE at the royal capital near present-day <b>Anyang</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Oracle-bone writing proved Yinxu at <b>Anyang</b> the last Shang capital, but no such evidence exists for earlier sites.</blockquote><a href="/culture/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>From about 1250 BCE diviners of the Shang kings near present-day <b>Anyang</b> asked the ancestors and gods about harvests, weather, war, hunting and childbirth, heated cattle shoulder blades and turtle shells, read the cracks and carved the questions on the bones.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li></ul></section>
